### How do you declare a generic sealed type?

A sealed interface can declare a type parameter. Each permitted subtype decides how to handle the parameter: keep it generic, bind it to a concrete type, or add bounds.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public sealed interface Order<T extends Instrument>
    permits BuyOrder, SellOrder {}

public record BuyOrder<T extends Instrument>(
    T instrument, BigDecimal quantity, BigDecimal price
) implements Order<T> {}

public record SellOrder<T extends Instrument>(
    T instrument, BigDecimal quantity, BigDecimal price
) implements Order<T> {}

public interface Instrument {
    String ticker();
}

public record Equity(String ticker, String exchange) implements Instrument {}
public record Bond(String ticker, String issuer) implements Instrument {}
```

### Why does the permits clause name classes, not types?

The `permits` clause lists class names like `BuyOrder` and `SellOrder`, not parameterized types like `BuyOrder<Equity>`. This is because generics are erased at runtime. The `PermittedSubclasses` attribute in the class file stores raw class names. The closed set is defined at the class level, independent of type arguments.

```java
// permits names classes:
public sealed interface Order<T extends Instrument>
    permits BuyOrder, SellOrder {}

// NOT parameterized types:
// permits BuyOrder<Equity>, SellOrder<Bond>  ← invalid syntax
```

### How does exhaustiveness work with generics?

An exhaustive `switch` over `Order<? extends Instrument>` only needs to cover `BuyOrder` and `SellOrder`. Exhaustiveness is checked against the closed set of permitted classes, not against the possible type arguments.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class OrderProcessor {
    public static <T extends Instrument> BigDecimal notional(Order<T> order) {
        return switch (order) {
            case BuyOrder<T> b  -> b.quantity().multiply(b.price());
            case SellOrder<T> s -> s.quantity().multiply(s.price());
            // No default: exhaustive over the two permitted classes
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var equity = new Equity("RELIANCE", "NSE");
        var buy = new BuyOrder<>(equity,
            new BigDecimal("100"), new BigDecimal("2450.75"));

        System.out.println("Notional: " + notional(buy));
    }
}
```

```text
Notional: 245075.00
```

### How do wildcards work with sealed generics?

Wildcards work exactly as they do for any generic type. You can accept `Order<? extends Instrument>` to read any order regardless of instrument.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class RiskEngine {
    // Accepts any order of any instrument
    public static BigDecimal grossValue(Order<? extends Instrument> order) {
        return switch (order) {
            case BuyOrder<?> b  -> b.quantity().multiply(b.price());
            case SellOrder<?> s -> s.quantity().multiply(s.price());
        };
    }
}
```

Note the `BuyOrder<?>` pattern: you use a wildcard because the type argument is erased at runtime.

### How do you model Result<T> with sealed generics?

Sealing plus generics is a clean way to build type-safe heterogeneous results.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public sealed interface Result<T> permits Success, Failure {}

public record Success<T>(T value) implements Result<T> {}
public record Failure<T>(String error, int code) implements Result<T> {}
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ResultDemo {
    public static Result<BigDecimal> getBalance(String accountId) {
        if (accountId == null || accountId.isBlank()) {
            return new Failure<>("Account ID required", 400);
        }
        return new Success<>(new BigDecimal("50000.00"));
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Result<BigDecimal> result = getBalance("RAJESH-001");

        String output = switch (result) {
            case Success<BigDecimal>(var balance)
                -> "Balance: " + balance;
            case Failure<BigDecimal>(var error, var code)
                -> "Error " + code + ": " + error;
        };

        System.out.println(output);
    }
}
```

```text
Balance: 50000.00
```

### How does a subtype fix the type parameter?

A permitted subtype can bind the type parameter to a concrete type, creating a specialized variant.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// General order interface
public sealed interface Order<T extends Instrument>
    permits BuyOrder, SellOrder, EquityMarketOrder {}

// Keeps generic
public record BuyOrder<T extends Instrument>(
    T instrument, BigDecimal quantity, BigDecimal price
) implements Order<T> {}

public record SellOrder<T extends Instrument>(
    T instrument, BigDecimal quantity, BigDecimal price
) implements Order<T> {}

// Fixes T to Equity
public record EquityMarketOrder(
    Equity instrument, BigDecimal quantity
) implements Order<Equity> {}
```

### How does bounded type propagation work?

Bounded type parameters on the sealed type propagate to permitted subtypes, keeping the domain consistent.

```java
// The bound T extends Instrument flows to all subtypes
// BuyOrder<String> would not compile because String
// does not extend Instrument
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

Sealing closes the family of classes; generics type the payload. Wildcards let you consume the whole family at the use site. The closed set and exhaustive switch hold regardless of type arguments. Variance choices still belong to the use site via wildcards; sealing does not change variance. Generic sealed hierarchies like `Result<T>` and `Order<T>` are powerful modeling tools because they combine type safety with compiler-checked exhaustiveness.
